  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n- Warm End to the Week with Morning Fog\n\nTranquil and relatively warm weather persists through Sunday \nunder a pronounced upper level ridge and large area of high \npressure. Ensemble systems are practically unified with the \ntemperature forecast through Sunday, with highs about 10 degrees \nabove normal, in the mid 60s to around 70.\n\nTrue to the season, areas of morning fog has been a challenge \nmuch of this week. For this morning, patchy dense fog has \ndeveloped between Whitehall and Ludington, while the air off Lake \nHuron invading Central Michigan has been more low stratus instead \nof dense fog, at least so far. Patchy fog will continue to be a \npossibility with radiational cooling in areas under clear skies \nthrough daybreak. There is another signal among the REFS member \nmodels for areas of fog to develop again tonight in much of \ncentral to south- central Michigan.\n\n- Cooler Monday-Tuesday Then Becoming Unsettled\n\nWith a flattening of the ridge early next week, a cold front \nassociated with a low pressure system tracking from the lee of the\nCanadian Rockies to Hudson Bay will likely bring cooler air into \nMichigan, and probably without much precip. Ensembles have more \nspread in temperature solutions for Monday-Tuesday, but the \nmajority do bring cooler air with highs within the 50s to mid 60s,\nwhich is closer to normal.\n\nThere is also some signal for temperatures to rebound into the \n60s again midweek, ahead of a low that would track from the Pac NW\nthrough the northern Plains toward the Great Lakes. This low \ncould bring the next best chance for about a quarter inch of \nrainfall around Thursday, though for now we are running with PoPs \naround 30 percent.\n\nThereafter, ensembles diverge greatly regarding the positions of \nthe large-scale northern stream trough and ridge over central \nNorth America. So this means low confidence in the forecast (for \nnow) for the end of next week, as model solutions really contain \nall combinations of warm and cold, dry and wet... but not cold \nenough for snow yet.",
